#: ../../operations.rst:2052
msgid "Replacing the binary without closing the port"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2055
msgid ""
"With ``global.reuse_port`` ``true`` we upgrade Sibuna, or change a "
"setting that needs a restart, without a moment in which the port is "
"closed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2057
msgid ""
"/opt/sibuna/new/sibuna check -f /etc/sibuna/edge.jlv\n"
"/opt/sibuna/new/sibuna --config-file /etc/sibuna/edge.jlv &   # binds the "
"same addresses\n"
"kill -TERM \"$OLD_PID\"                                         # drains, "
"then stops"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2063
msgid ""
"The new process binds the same addresses while the old one serves, and "
"the kernel gives each new connection to one of them. On SIGTERM the old "
"process stops accepting and waits up to ``shutdown_timeout`` for its "
"established flows. The new process takes the admin socket when it starts, "
"and the old process leaves that socket alone when it stops."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2065
msgid ""
"A takeover needs no ``global.drain_delay``: nothing in front has to "
"notice, because the new process already accepts. With a delay the old "
"process keeps accepting its share of new connections for that time, and "
"only then stops. We set a delay for the other case, a node that leaves "
"the pool of a balancer in front."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2067
msgid ""
"A connection that reaches the old process in the instant it closes its "
"listener is reset, and the client connects again. In our test with a "
"client that opens a new connection for every request, no request failed "
"in 15 takeovers. An HTTP/3 connection does not survive the takeover: the "
"kernel may hand its packets to the other process, which does not know the "
"connection, and the client then connects again. Connections over TCP are "
"not affected. Without ``reuse_port`` a second process on the same address "
"is refused with ADDRESS IN USE, which protects against starting the "
"service twice by mistake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2069
msgid "Replacing the binary without losing a connection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2072
msgid ""
"With ``global.reuse_port`` the old process has listeners of its own until "
"it exits. While it drains, the kernel still gives it a share of the new "
"connections, and it refuses them. In our test, with one long stream that "
"held the old process in its drain for 1.7 s, 10,000 to 22,000 connections "
"failed in that time. UDP clients were given a server again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2074
msgid ""
"With ``global.upgrade_socket`` the old process gives its listening "
"sockets to the new one. There is one accept queue, and it is never "
"closed. The old process also gives its UDP mappings, so a UDP client "
"keeps its server, and the server sees the same source address. The key "
"needs Linux."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2076
msgid ""
"global: {\n"
"    upgrade_socket: \"/run/sibuna/edge.upgrade\",\n"
"},"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2082
msgid ""
"/opt/sibuna/new/sibuna check -f /etc/sibuna/edge.jlv\n"
"/opt/sibuna/new/sibuna --config-file /etc/sibuna/edge.jlv    # replaces "
"the running daemon"
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2087
msgid ""
"The command of an upgrade is the command of a start. A process that "
"starts with this key connects to the path. When a daemon answers there, "
"the new process takes its place. When nobody answers, it starts as a "
"first process does. We do not signal the old process. It exits by itself "
"with status 0 when its established flows have ended, or after "
"``shutdown_timeout``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2089
msgid ""
"new process                                old process\n"
"-----------                                -----------\n"
"reads and checks its file                  serves\n"
"connects, takes the listening sockets      serves\n"
"starts everything, reads nothing yet       serves\n"
"says that it is ready  ------------------> stops reading UDP, still "
"accepts TCP\n"
"                       <------------------ gives the UDP mappings\n"
"accepts TCP, acknowledges  --------------> stops accepting, drains, exits "
"0\n"
"reads UDP, \"listeners ready\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../operations.rst:2101
msgid ""
"A new process that fails leaves the old one serving. This holds for a "
"file that does not check, for a certificate or a log file that cannot be "
"opened, and for a process that dies or hangs before it acknowledges. The "
"old process waits 10 s for the acknowledgement, then ends the new process "
"with SIGKILL and reads UDP again. Datagrams that arrived meanwhile waited "
"in the socket."
msgstr ""

